      "user-1": {
        "kind": "user",
        "id": "user-1",
        "text": "Explain the difference between a process and a thread. Use a heading, a bulleted list, a small table, and one short TypeScript code block.",
        "images": [],
        "to": null,
        "queue": null
      },
      "text-2": {
        "kind": "text",
        "id": "text-2",
        "markdown": "# Process vs. Thread\n\n## Core Difference\n\n- **Process:** An independent running program with its own memory space, file handles, and resources. The operating system isolates it from other processes.\n- **Thread:** A unit of execution *inside* a process. Threads in the same process share its memory and resources but have their own call stack and register state.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- A process is the unit of **resource ownership**; a thread is the unit of **scheduling**.\n- Creating a process is expensive because the OS must set up a new address space. Creating a thread is cheap.\n- Threads communicate by reading and writing shared memory, which requires synchronization (locks, atomics) to avoid races.\n- Processes communicate through IPC mechanisms such as pipes, sockets, or shared memory segments, which are safer but slower.\n- A crash in one thread can take down the whole process. A crash in one process leaves others running.\n\n## Comparison\n\n| Aspect | Process | Thread |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory | Separate address space | Shared with sibling threads |\n| Creation cost | High | Low |\n| Isolation | Strong | Weak |\n| Communication | IPC (pipes, sockets) | Shared memory |\n| Context switch | Slower | Faster |\n\n## Example\n\nNode.js runs JavaScript on a single thread within one process. Worker threads can be started for CPU-heavy work, and they share memory only through explicitly transferred or shared buffers:\n\n```typescript\nimport { Worker } from \"node:worker_threads\";\n\n// Runs in a separate thread, inside the same process\nconst worker = new Worker(new URL(\"./hash-worker.js\", import.meta.url), {\n  workerData: { password: \"hunter2\" },\n});\n\nworker.on(\"message\", (hash: string) => console.log(\"hash:\", hash));\n```",
        "streaming": false
      },
